C = [
("h1", "13. The Father and the Son"),
("p", "Claim: Paul names the Father and the Son together in nearly every letter, and he never merges them. The sayings that call Christ God are made by a writer who also writes of “God and the Father” and of the Son as another. The chapter reads the sentences in which the two stand together, so that the volume does not claim more than the sentences allow."),
("sub", "13.1 The opening and closing words of the letters"),
("p", "Paul’s greetings name both. “Grace be unto you, and peace, from God our Father and the Lord Jesus Christ” (Colossians 1:2). “From God the Father and the Lord Jesus Christ our Saviour” (Titus 1:4). The next verse of Colossians adds, “We give thanks to God and the Father of our Lord Jesus Christ” (Colossians 1:3). In each the Father is God and the Son is Lord, and they are named in one sentence, with one grace and one peace coming from the two. The sayings of the earlier chapters are made by the writer of these greetings."),
("sub", "13.2 The hymn and the Father’s glory"),
("p", "The hymn of Philippians ends, “And that every tongue should confess that Jesus Christ is Lord, to the glory of God the Father” (Philippians 2:11). The confession of the Son is directed to the glory of the Father. The earlier verse says that “God also hath highly exalted him” (Philippians 2:9), so that the one who exalts is God and the one exalted is the one who was in the form of God. The hymn does not use two vocabularies for two Gods. It puts the one who was in the form of God and the one who exalted him in a single drama and gives each his part."),
("sub", "13.3 The Father’s pleasure and the Son’s fulness"),
("p", "Colossians 1:19 says, “For it pleased the Father that in him should all fulness dwell.” The Father is the one whose pleasure it was, and the Son is the one in whom the fulness dwells. The same letter says at 2:9 that the fulness is of the Godhead. A reader who finds only the first verse might say that the Son is less than the Father, since the Father gave. A reader who finds only the second might say that the Father and the Son are not distinct. Paul wrote both. The volume reports the pair and does not resolve it."),
("sub", "13.4 “The Son also himself be subject”"),
("p", "The passage that most plainly sets the Son beneath the Father is in 1 Corinthians 15. “Then cometh the end, when he shall have delivered up the kingdom to God, even the Father; when he shall have put down all rule and all authority and power. For he must reign, till he hath put all enemies under his feet” (1 Corinthians 15:24, 25). And at the end: “And when all things shall be subdued unto him, then shall the Son also himself be subject unto him that put all things under him, that God may be all in all” (1 Corinthians 15:28)."),
("p", "The verse is sometimes used to deny that the Son is God, and sometimes ignored by those who hold that he is. The volume reports it. It says the Son is subject to the Father at the end, and that the end follows his reign over all enemies. It does not say that the subjection is a lesser nature, and it does not say that the subjection cancels what Colossians 1 and 2 say of the fulness. The resurrection order and the end of the reign are the subject of the volume on that order, and the volume on the Kingdom Program reads the reign. Here the verse is cited for one point: Paul keeps the Father and the Son distinct in the same letter in which he says that “by whom are all things” (1 Corinthians 8:6) is the Lord Jesus Christ."),
("sub", "13.5 The image of God in Second Corinthians"),
("p", "Paul uses the word “image” of Christ in another letter. The god of this world has blinded the minds of unbelievers, “lest the light of the glorious gospel of Christ, who is the image of God, should shine unto them” (2 Corinthians 4:4). A few verses later: “For God, who commanded the light to shine out of darkness, hath shined in our hearts, to give the light of the knowledge of the glory of God in the face of Jesus Christ” (2 Corinthians 4:6). The word of Colossians 1:15 appears, with a different setting. In Colossians the image is set against rival teachers. In 2 Corinthians it is set against the blindness of unbelief. The knowledge of the glory of God is in the face of Jesus Christ, and the sentence does not say that there is another face."),
("ctbl", "The Father and the Son in the sentences of Paul.", [
    ("The passage", "How the two are named", "What it leaves unsaid"),
    ("Colossians 1:2, 3", "God our Father and the Lord Jesus Christ; God and the Father of our Lord Jesus Christ", "How the one grace comes from two"),
    ("Titus 1:4", "God the Father and the Lord Jesus Christ our Saviour", "Why Saviour is used of both in this letter"),
    ("Philippians 2:9–11", "God exalted him; every tongue confesses Jesus Christ is Lord, to the glory of God the Father", "How the glory of the Father and the lordship of the Son stand together"),
    ("Colossians 1:19", "It pleased the Father that in him should all fulness dwell", "The sense of “pleased”; the verse does not define it"),
    ("1 Corinthians 8:6", "One God, the Father, of whom are all things; one Lord Jesus Christ, by whom are all things", "How “of whom” and “by whom” divide the work"),
    ("1 Corinthians 15:24–28", "The kingdom delivered up to God, even the Father; the Son also himself subject", "What the subjection means for the Son’s nature"),
    ("2 Corinthians 4:4, 6", "Christ the image of God; the glory of God in the face of Jesus Christ", "How the image and the glory are one"),
  ], [0.24, 0.42, 0.34]),
("sub", "13.6 What the volume takes from the chapter"),
("bul", [
    "Paul names the Father and the Son together in his greetings and never merges them.",
    "The hymn gives the exalting to God and the lordship to Jesus Christ, to the glory of God the Father.",
    "Colossians 1:19 gives the pleasure to the Father and the fulness to the Son, and 2:9 gives the fulness as the Godhead’s.",
    "First Corinthians 15:28 says the Son also shall be subject to the Father at the end, and the volume does not read it as a denial of the fulness.",
    "The word image is used of Christ in Colossians 1:15 and 2 Corinthians 4:4, in two different settings.",
  ]),
("p", "The Father and the Son are two in the sentences and one in the sayings of Philippians, Colossians, 1 Timothy and Titus. The volume does not say how. The next chapters gather what has been read."),
]
